WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:03.203
Where does AI in rural health stand today,

00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:07.620
where is it heading, and how can it
actually help? Let's start with today.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.065
In 2023, about half of rural hospitals
used AI or other prediction tools,

00:00:13.065 --> 00:00:16.339
compared with about four
in five urban hospitals.

00:00:16.339 --> 00:00:21.643
Rural hospitals were also less likely to
check those tools for accuracy or bias,

00:00:21.643 --> 00:00:24.458
and 16 percent of rural respondents didn't

00:00:24.458 --> 00:00:27.870
know whether their
hospital used them at all.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:32.205
Many tools are also built with
patients from somewhere else.

00:00:32.205 --> 00:00:34.470
In one review of medical-imaging AI,

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:38.878
most studies used patients from
California, Massachusetts, or New York.

00:00:38.878 --> 00:00:44.360
No patient groups at all could be traced
to thirty-four states, including Alabama.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.465
And a tool that works well in testing
can still struggle in a real clinic.

00:00:49.465 --> 00:00:52.949
When an eye-screening AI was
used in clinics in Thailand,

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:56.553
about one in five photos was
rejected as too poor to grade,

00:00:56.553 --> 00:00:58.174
often because of lighting.

00:00:58.174 --> 00:01:01.418
On slow internet,
some uploads took a minute or more,

00:01:01.418 --> 00:01:03.800
so fewer patients were screened.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:09.460
Tomorrow is arriving fast. In 2024,
about one in four rural hospitals already

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.314
used generative AI in
their health records,

00:01:12.314 --> 00:01:15.558
compared with about one
in three urban hospitals.

00:01:15.558 --> 00:01:18.607
More than half of rural
hospitals had no plans

00:01:18.607 --> 00:01:22.510
to start within the next year,
so the gap could grow.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:28.014
So where can AI help most? Three places:
less paperwork, like sorting referrals

00:01:28.014 --> 00:01:31.508
or preparing insurance claims;
expertise closer to home,

00:01:31.508 --> 00:01:34.267
like eye screening in
a primary care office;

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:38.590
and better planning,
like spotting patients who need follow-up.

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:43.928
But good tools don't always stick.
Research on health technology found that

00:01:43.928 --> 00:01:46.921
when a program is complex
in many ways at once,

00:01:46.921 --> 00:01:50.100
for patients, for staff,
and for the organization,

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.680
it rarely, if ever,
becomes part of everyday care.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.474
That's why local testing comes first.
We call it scenario testing.

00:01:58.474 --> 00:02:01.603
Pick realistic local cases,
including messy ones.

00:02:01.603 --> 00:02:03.982
Decide what a good result looks like.

00:02:03.982 --> 00:02:07.111
Run the tool quietly,
before anyone relies on it.

00:02:07.111 --> 00:02:09.890
And keep checking after it goes live.

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:15.032
AI can help rural care, but only if
it's tested where it will be used.

00:02:15.032 --> 00:02:18.310
Next: which data you
can safely use with AI.
